<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theobald II, Count of Champagne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1090–<a href="/years/1152">1152</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Theobald IV of Blois, Theobald the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theobald II, called the Great, was count of Champagne from 1125 and, as Theobald IV, count of Blois and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> from 1102. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-count-of-blois">Stephen-Henry</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adela-of-normandy">Adela of Normandy</a> and brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england">Stephen, King of England</a>, he reunited Blois and Champagne. He supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cistercians">Cistercians</a> and encouraged the Champagne fairs, and on his death his lands were divided among his sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theobald-ii-count-of-champagne.jpg" alt="Theobald II, Count of Champagne" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri d'Arbois de Jubainville · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thibaut_Champagne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
